  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2018-08-21T00:00:54.243Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "As the majority of us already know, VRChat limits *only* polygons. Anyone who hasn't ever done a thing or two with game development or real time rendering will know that Polygons barely create any lag at all. Its almost completely unnoticeable infact. \n\nSo lets get on to it, here's what I suggest we do to \"Limit\" the creative process of users who don't understand how to optimize.\n\n- Increase the poly limit to 80,000 at the very least, the majority of models imported are generally 80K or less. This would be a comfortable zone.\n\n- Add a material limit, and make it 5.\n\nNow most people will tell me \"Why 5? my model has hundreds, how am I going to make this work?\" and let me stop you right there, the amount of materials on a model will create extreme lag for other users, the more materials, the more draw calls are needed, and that uses up more of the cpu/gpu. (That's the short version of it anyway)\n\nIf someone can't atlas the materials down in blender, or get as few materials as humanly possible on the model, then they shouldn't use that model period. It takes only a few minutes at most to do this, and doesn't require much effort. \n\n- Add a cloth limit of 3 clothed parts.\n\n- Only allow a max of 4 meshes on the model.\n\nMore meshes require more materials, and that in turn creates more draw calls, join all meshes up besides the head and weapon(s) so everyone else has an easier time loading your model.\n\n- Limit dynamic bones to only 3 components. \n\nBy now, people should know how to make a dynamic bone root to affect most parts of the hair or clothes they use it on, there is absolutely no need to have more then 3 dynamic bone components.\n\nAnd that's that, let me know what you guys think.",
